            <ArticleTitle>MC4R expression in pedunculopontine nucleus involved in the modulation of midbrain dopamine system.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2039-43</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Separate studies have implicated the pedunculopontine tegmental nucleus (PPTg) in processing aversive stimuli to dopamine systems, and melanocortin-4 receptor (MC4R) are broadly expressed by the neurons in the PPTg, but the exact neurosubstrate underlying the regulation of dopamine systems by the central melanocortin pathway is poorly understood.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this study, the PPTg of 6 adult mice expressing green fluorescent protein (GFP) under the control of the MC4R promoter was detected by fluorescence immunohistochemistry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A large number of GFP-positive neurons in the dissipated parts of PPTg (dpPPTg) were found, and approximately 50% of MC4R-GFP- positive neurons in the dpPPTg coexpressed tyrosine hydroxylase, a marker of dopamine neurons, indicating that they were dopaminergic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our findings support the hypothesis that MC4R signaling in the dpPPTg may involve in the modulation of midbrain dopamine systems.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
